<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brussels</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bruxelles, Brussel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brussels is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the seat of the main institutions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s headquarters. It grew from a settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senne">Senne</a>, fortified by the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a> in the 11th century, into a residence of the dukes of Brabant and the Burgundian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. A revolt in Brussels in August 1830 launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brussels.jpg" alt="Brussels" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Conde Sánchez · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Place_Bruselas_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leopold II ruled the Congo Free State for twenty-three years without ever setting foot in it, governing by decree from <b>Brussels</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Burgundian and Habsburg rule the county kept its own institutions, its sovereign court at Mons and its customary law, codified in the general charters of 1619, while its rulers governed the Netherlands as a whole through governors and councils in <b>Brussels</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Brabant grew out of the County of Louvain, a lordship on the Dyle river held from the eleventh century by a line of counts who also controlled <b>Brussels</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Archduke Ferdinand of Austria, younger brother of the Emperor Charles V, had received the Austrian hereditary lands in the partition agreements of Worms (1521) and <b>Brussels</b> (1522), and in 1521 he married Anna of Bohemia and Hungary, while his sister Mary married her brother, King Louis II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Belgium’s line from <b>Brussels</b> to Mechelen, opened in May 1835, was the first steam passenger railway in continental Europe, built and run by the state itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>European powers cited the suppression of the slave trade to justify conquests in Africa, as at the <b>Brussels</b> Anti-Slavery Conference of 1889–1890, while imposing forced labour of their own.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
